reen  they'll be ripe within a couple of weeks  we see some here on raspberries that  will start blooming a little bit later  in the season and we'll have fruit into  the fall  so we ceased berry production in new  york from the early part of june the  late part of may early part of june  through  december in parts of the state and with  high tunnels that we're that we're  standing under and low tunnels for  strawberries we can really extend that  strawberry and raspberry season into  different times of the year in new york  so i work on as i said three different  species  four different species actually  but three different crops so how's that  possible so we have strawberry of course  that's its own species and we have  summer strawberries which are ripe now  and then there's day neutral  strawberries as well that produce late  in the in the season in the summer and  into the fall  in raspberry we have red raspberry and  we have black raspberry and there are  different species  both are native to this part of the  world and so there's a strong tradition  of these crops being grown and consumed  in this area  red raspberry then has a summer season  where you see the fruit developing now  it also has a fall season was where we  get flowers  in mid-summer and then fruit into the  fall  in black raspberry traditionally it has  been a summer season only  and that's one of the things that we are  doing is transferring that fall bearing  trait from red raspberry into black  raspberry so that will have two seasons  for for black raspberry as well  blackberry has never been a large part  of the program uh this the winters here  tend to be very difficult for  blackberries to survive and we see a lot  of winter damage we did have a mild  winter last year so you can see we did  get very good survival and a lot of  bloom this year but some years we see uh  up to 90 cane death in the blackberries  and we don't get any summer crop so  towards that end we're actually  working with some of the new material  mostly developed at university of  arkansas for primocane or fall bearing  blackberries as well and so to get  fall bearing blackberries that are  adapted to new york that can handle our  winters  as a plant and to handle our summers as  well oddly enough blackberries are tend  to be sensitive to high temperatures  during bloom um so we're looking for  blackberries that will bloom in the  warmest part of the year and produce  fruit in the fall before it gets cold  and and freezes  so it's a pretty mixed bag when it comes  to genetics we have the  red and black raspberries are diploid  the blackberries are tetraploid  and the strawberries are octoploid so  we're working in multiple ploidy levels  across the different species  some of the technology that we're using  in breeding first of all was the high  tunnels  seems  to be a fairly simple technology but  it's a tremendous technology when it  comes to raspberry and blackberry fruit  quality  worldwide most of the raspberries for  fresh consumption that you see in the  supermarket are grown under high tunnels  and it's to keep the rain off we're out  here today on a bit misty and windy  rainy day  and the plants are dry and the fruit is  dry and we're not getting spread of  fungus so botrytis gray mold is the most  common fungus  post harvest fungus that we see in  raspberries and it really causes a lot  of damage to the fruit and so  keeping the fruit dry  keeps the fruit from getting infected  and increases our shelf life by  two three four hundred percent  and so we're looking at instead of  having shelf life of two to three days  for red raspberries we can see a shelf  life of seven to ten days and so that  really makes a huge difference when it  comes to marketing if you're going to  grow the fruit in a concentrated area  like california for the most part  and ship them across the country  you've got to have a lot of shelf life  and we see the same thing in new york  we have a lot of rainy days in the  summertime and if we want to have good  quality  locally produced fruit we need to keep  that fruit dry and so we instituted  high tunnels in our program in 2008 we  were we produced or we set up the first  high tunnels here at agritech  and since then agritech has installed  more high tunnels to work with cherries  to work with tomatoes strawberries other  vegetable crops and now hemp  and so we're seeing  high tunnels being recognized as a very  important technology  for production and of course if you're  going to produce them  in this type of system you should breed  them in this type of system so that they  are adapted to this environment  more recently we've added low tunnels to  the mix for strawberries so we see the  same benefits for tunnels with  strawberries  as we do in high tunnels for raspberries  keeping the rain off increases the shelf  life of the berries improves the quality  raspberries are strawberries are in  full harvest right now in new york and  as you can imagine wet berries don't  store very well and so keeping them dry  until the rain passes so that we can  harvest them dry really makes a big  difference in fruit quality and so we're  breeding varieties under those low  tunnels so that they are adapted that to  that environment because the environment  is different under these conditions we  see different pest pressure  we see different insects and diseases  that can be a problem on the inside  under these tunnels than what we see in  the field and so we want to be sure that  we know what problems growers might have  and how to address them  in we also have a lab program where we  are working on various aspects of  genomics in in raspberry for the most  part  our most uh recent project has been  looking at thornlessness in in red  raspberry and in blackberry  so in red in red raspberry black  raspberry and blackberry in the wild all  the plants have thorns so the wild type  is a thorny or spiny  is a spiny cane but what you see in  blackberries is the thorns can be very  very stout  and they will penetrate your skin very  easily they're very very sharp we see  the same thing in black raspberry  very sharp thorns very stout often  they're a little bit curved  and they will stick into your skin and  penetrate your skin very easily so it  makes harvest very difficult makes  pruning very difficult it really makes  working with the plants very difficult  well in blackberry and red raspberry  there have been natural mutants found in  the wild that are spine free  and so that has been a big boon to the  industry to make spine-free varieties  because you can see as i'm handling this  cane i don't have to be very careful  they're not gonna there's no spines on  here to to cause damage and this is a  blackberry cane without thorns  and so there are natural mutants that we  have been using and breeding to produce  thornless varieties and that's really a  must especially in blackberry because  they're so so stout  in black raspberry unfortunately we  haven't been able to find any natural  mutants in the wild but you can cross  black raspberry and red raspberry and  what happens when you cross those two  species is you get purple raspberry  and so it takes some generations to get  some some black fruit back out of that  but we've been able to transfer that  thornless trait from red raspberry into  black raspberry background and now we  have spine free black raspberry canes  and our next varieties our newest  varieties of black raspberry will be  spine free  we're also transferring that that trait  of primocane fruiting into black  raspberry so we hope to have soon  black raspberries that are both  primocane fruiting so fall fruiting and  spine free as well as spine free summer  fruiting black raspberries and so that's  a pretty important trait and so what  we've been doing as well  in the lab is examining that at the  genetic level to determine what gene is  actually causing uh the spineless trait  to come through so we used a population  that was segregating for this trait  and we examined the the genome of the  progenitor parents and that's offspring  and located a region  um  in the black in the red raspberry genome  where this where the single gene for  spineless keynes uh resides and we have  several candidate genes that we've we've  identified that we think uh one of them  is actually the gene involved and so  that's where we're at at this stage  we've published some of this work  on that and we're  going to continue working on this to see  if we can actually use this gene once we  find out the exact sequence  to potentially change some of the  varieties that are already out there  using gene editing techniques into  spineless  or spine free  varieties and so some varieties that we  already know are well adapted and that  everybody  already is growing might be a little  easier to grow if they don't have spines  and so that's kind of the some of the  stuff that we're looking at into the  future for  new varieties  so one of the trends we're seeing in  raspberry around the world for  commercial production is substrate  cultures growing this the plants in bags  or in pots  to take away some of the variability of  soil conditions  in many places of the world soils are  not really suitable for raspberry and so  to have a more uniform  growing environment we're seeing more  substrate culture so what we see  around me and behind me are raspberry  seedlings actually in substrate so we  decided a few years ago that if some of  the raspberry varieties are going to be  grown in substrate we should probably be  breeding some varieties for that  environment and in that environment so  we've what we have here are raspberry  seedlings in pots these seedlings as you  can see some of them are already several  feet tall some of them are even three or  four or five feet tall  they were started from seed this year so  these plants are all of a couple of  months old so you can see how fast they  grow  raspberry seed is no bigger than a  mustard seed it's very small  but they can produce a very large plant  very quickly about 75 percent of these  plants will produce fruit this year and  so we're we're able to accelerate the  breeding process by putting them in pots  and growing them in the greenhouse  starting in february and march so that  we can have plants in the in the field  in may and actually produce fruit in  july august  and through the end of the year and so  this allows us to accelerate the  breeding process we can evaluate fruit  very quickly  from seed if we were to do this in the  open field it takes at least two years  for us to get a mature plant or we can  see fruit so in this case we've cut off  at least a year from the breeding  process  or even more because once we have this  plant  these plants are very clean in here  they're very low disease incidents so we  have a  selection that looks very promising we  can go straight to tissue culture from  this plant  and we can start producing plantlets for  trials  within months of the seed being  germinated  in the field that's a two or three year  process just to get to the point where  we might start tissue culture  and it could be four or five years and  so we can cut off multiple years from  the breeding process while also  developing varieties that might be  suitable for substrate culture and so we  have partners in new york the growers we  partner with the new york state berry  growers association association to um  to test  potential new varieties around the state  but we also have partners around this  around the u.s and around the world that  also test our new material to see if  it's suitable for different areas around  the world  for commercial production and so this is  one of the things that we instituted a  few years so we can fit about 1200  seedlings in this one high tunnel and we  can cycle through those seedlings each  year so in a three year period  where we might plant in the field those  3 600 plants we can go through 3 600  plants here in this high tunnel  in the field that would take  about four acres  to cover and in here we're doing it in  about  a tenth of an acre and so it really  reduces the amount of inputs reduces the  amount of time that we have the plants  in the field and really is increasing  our efficiency in moving through the  germplasm  one of the things that also allows us to  do is to test for some of the diseases  that we see in high tunnels that we  don't see in the field very much and so  this environment is very conducive to  powdery mildew it can be a real problem  in raspberry and so we can see powdery  mildew susceptibility in that first year  in the seedlings and eliminate those  types of germ plasms uh from our our  program and try to produce varieties  that are only resistant to pottery  mildew  you can see here some rat strawberries  you see our low tunnels it's a nice  rainy day here so you can see what why  we use them keeps the fruit dry keeps  the plants healthy you can see in here  the  the foliage is beautiful and clean  there's very little disease you can see  the fruit ripening here on the on the  plant these are seedlings in the  breeding program so these are not  varieties some of them are  pretty impressive and some of them not  so impressive that's typical  we can see some nice fruit coming off  here we're looking for in strawberry you  can see this nice pretty calyx  nice shiny fruit this one's a little uh  i don't know if you can see but the  seeds are a little sunken we don't like  that because that makes the skin really  easy to bruise  and so that's a trait we look at in  particular in strawberry  something that maybe the consumer  wouldn't really recognize we want the  seeds and the flesh to be even so that  that  you don't you can see the juice on my  fingers it's just just touching it's  enough to damage that fruit and of  course that won't pack well it won't  ship well and so that's something we  really look at you see we want good size  we want big fruit everybody likes big  fruit the pictures like big fruit  because it fills containers quickly  see a lot of color differences in  strawberry  if you look at just these three here  anything from the deep deep red to this  kind of shiny red to this pale  orange color and this is kind of what  we're seeing in the open market is that  paler color  not much interior flesh color  in the supermarket that fruit often  looks very fresh because of the  fluorescent lights the local consumer  is more  used to a darker colored berry  they see that as something that's a  little more ripe a little fresher  and that's a way we can distinguish our  local fruit from the supermarket  wholesale fruit is just by color  often texture as well our varieties are  generally softer than what you'll find  in the supermarket because they don't  have to be shipped for seven days and so  we can get away with something that's a  little bit softer it gives a little bit  better  eating experience where they get a lot  more juice in their mouth  and consumers really enjoy that fresh  local produce that comes right from the  field today maybe the same day in many  cases  and you really can't get much better  than that than a strawberry in june in  new york  this has been a production of cornell  university on the web at cornell.edu

