reen  they'll be ripe within a couple of weeks  we see some here on raspberries that  will start blooming a little bit later  in the season and we'll have fruit into  the fall  so we ceased berry production in new  york from the early part of june the  late part of may early part of june  through  december in parts of the state and with  high tunnels that we're that we're  standing under and low tunnels for  strawberries we can really extend that  strawberry and raspberry season into  different times of the year in new york  so i work on as i said three different  species  four different species actually  but three different crops so how's that  possible so we have strawberry of course  that's its own species and we have  summer strawberries which are ripe now  and then there's day neutral  strawberries as well that produce late  in the in the season in the summer and  into the fall  in raspberry we have red raspberry and  we have black raspberry and there are  different species  both are native to this part of the  world and so there's a strong tradition  of these crops being grown and consumed  in this area  red raspberry then has a summer season  where you see the fruit developing now  it also has a fall season was where we  get flowers  in mid-summer and then fruit into the  fall  in black raspberry traditionally it has  been a summer season only  and that's one of the things that we are  doing is transferring that fall bearing  trait from red raspberry into black  raspberry so that will have two seasons  for for black raspberry as well  blackberry has never been a large part  of the program uh this the winters here  tend to be very difficult for  blackberries to survive and we see a lot  of winter damage we did have a mild  winter last year so you can see we did  get very good survival and a lot of  bloom this year but some years we see uh  up to 90 cane death in the blackberries  and we don't get any summer crop so  towards that end we're actually  working with some of the new material  mostly developed at university of  arkansas for primocane or fall bearing  blackberries as well and so to get  fall bearing blackberries that are  adapted to new york that can handle our  winters  as a plant and to handle our summers as  well oddly enough blackberries are tend  to be sensitive to high temperatures  during bloom um so we're looking for  blackberries that will bloom in the  warmest part of the year and produce  fruit in the fall before it gets cold  and and freezes  so it's a pretty mixed bag when it comes  to genetics we have the  red and black raspberries are diploid  the blackberries are tetraploid  and the strawberries are octoploid so  we're working in multiple ploidy levels  across the different species  some of the technology that we're using  in breeding first of all was the high  tunnels  seems  to be a fairly simple technology but  it's a tremendous technology when it  comes to raspberry and blackberry fruit  quality  worldwide most of the raspberries for  fresh consumption that you see in the  supermarket are grown under high tunnels  and it's to keep the rain off we're out  here today on a bit misty and windy  rainy day  and the plants are dry and the fruit is  dry and we're not getting spread of  fungus so botrytis gray mold is the most  common fungus  post harvest fungus that we see in  raspberries and it really causes a lot  of damage to the fruit and so  keeping the fruit dry  keeps the fruit from getting infected  and increases our shelf life by  two three four hundred percent  and so we're looking at instead of  having shelf life of two to three days  for red raspberries we can see a shelf  life of seven to ten days and so that  really makes a huge difference when it  comes to marketing if you're going to  grow the fruit in a concentrated area  like california for the most part  and ship them across the country  you've got to have a lot of shelf life  and we see the same thing in new york  we have a lot of rainy days in the  summertime and if we want to have good  quality  locally produced fruit we need to keep  that fruit dry and so we instituted  high tunnels in our program in 2008 we  were we produced or we set up the first  high tunnels here at agritech  and since then agritech has installed  more high tunnels to work with cherries  to work with tomatoes strawberries other  vegetable crops and now hemp  and so we're seeing  high tunnels being recognized as a very  important technology  for production and of course if you're  going to produce them  in this type of system you should breed  them in this type of system so that they  are adapted to this environment  more recently we've added low tunnels to  the mix for strawberries so we see the  same benefits for tunnels with  strawberries  as we do in high tunnels for raspberries  keeping the rain off increases the shelf  life of the berries improves the quality  raspberries are strawberries are in  full harvest right now in new york and  as you can imagine wet berries don't  store very well and so keeping them dry  until the rain passes so that we can  harvest them dry really makes a big  difference in fruit quality and so we're  breeding varieties under those low  tunnels so that they are adapted that to  that environment because the environment  is different under these conditions we  see different pest pressure  we see different insects and diseases  that can be a problem on the inside  under these tunnels than what we see in  the field and so we want to be sure that  we know what problems growers might have  and how to address them  in we also have a lab program where we  are working on various aspects of  genomics in in raspberry for the most  part  our most uh recent project has been  looking at thornlessness in in red  raspberry and in blackberry  so in red in red raspberry black  raspberry and blackberry in the wild all  the plants have thorns so the wild type  is a thorny or spiny  is a spiny cane but what